// SOS M5 unit 6, THE NEGATIVE: THE KERNEL NEVER SHARES SLAB BACKING WITH
// USER-REACHABLE MEMORY (sawos design 32, on design 25 D-6).
//
// The transcript:
//
//   SOS slabshared: minted a sibling
//   SOS: process fault: object in the wrong state process=0x...
//   SOS: process teardown handles=...
//
// **THE SAFETY CONDITION IS `refs == 1 && maps == 0`, and this case exercises
// the first half.** A donated region becomes kernel object storage — the kernel
// writes Waiter slots into those bytes — so anything else that can still reach
// them is a hole straight through the object model. `refs` counts the HANDLE
// ENTRIES naming a region, so the donated handle being the ONLY one is exactly
// the question "can anybody else still name this?", and unit 5 is what made it
// askable (design 28 put both counts on the `MemorySlot`).
//
// A sibling is the cheapest way to make the condition false and the most honest:
// `mint` is the ordinary way a second name onto one region comes to exist, and
// nothing about the sibling is malformed — it is a perfectly good capability
// that simply must not coexist with a donation.
//
// WHY THE PROCESS DIES rather than getting a status back (design 178's faults
// ruling): root MINTED that sibling. A caller that cannot see a condition it
// created itself is not a caller the kernel can help, and every other
// caller-checkable refusal on this path — a handle that names nothing, a handle
// that is not a Memory, an unknown `SlabKind` — ends the process for the same
// reason. The two refusals root genuinely cannot compute (a region too small for
// one slot of some kind whose size is kernel-internal, an extent chain that is
// full) are statuses instead, and `tests/slab-donate` is where the op returns.
//
// The `maps == 0` half is NOT exercised here: proving it wants a live mapping,
// which wants a second process to map into or a self-map, and it is the same
// `BadState` out of the same three-condition block two lines below the one this
// case reaches. Design 32's As-built records that gap by name rather than
// claiming coverage this case does not have.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, SlabKind, SosStatus}

/// One protection unit's worth on either profile.
static PAGE: UInt = 0x1000

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabshared: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var pool_record = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabshared: no pool region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let pool = pool_record.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabshared: pool record is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    let scratch = try pool.split(len: PAGE) catch {
        print("SOS slabshared: no page to donate: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // THE SECOND NAME. `sibling` stays live for the rest of this program, which
    // is the point — a sibling that had been released would leave `refs` at 1
    // and the donation would be perfectly safe.
    let sibling = try scratch.mint() catch {
        print("SOS slabshared: no sibling: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS slabshared: minted a sibling\n")

    // THIS CALL DOES NOT RETURN. Both arms below are unreachable and say so, on
    // `tests/map-wx-refused`'s pattern: a case whose negative arrives as a fault
    // must fail loudly if it ever arrives as a value instead.
    match system.slab_donate(kind: SlabKind.Waiters, memory: move scratch) {
        case Ok(_) -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print(
                "SOS slabshared: UNREACHABLE — a shared region was absorbed\n")
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        },
        case Err(_) -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print(
                "SOS slabshared: UNREACHABLE — refused with a status\n")
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        },
    }
}
